WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.709
A number can look precise while answering
the wrong question.

2
00:00:05.709 --> 00:00:11.840
Example Station has observed rain of ten
millimetres yesterday and forecast rain

3
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:13.373
of twenty tomorrow.

4
00:00:13.373 --> 00:00:15.416
Today's reading is missing.

5
00:00:15.416 --> 00:00:18.993
Keep those periods and evidence types
separate.

6
00:00:18.993 --> 00:00:23.080
Thirty millimetres received would be an
unsupported summary.

7
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:29.786
Our invented feed worksheet starts with
one thousand kilograms of dry matter,

8
00:00:29.786 --> 00:00:34.708
adds one hundred and subtracts five
hundred of expected use.

9
00:00:34.708 --> 00:00:37.169
The arithmetic gives six hundred.

10
00:00:37.169 --> 00:00:39.137
Check the sum yourself.

11
00:00:39.137 --> 00:00:45.043
Then ask whether the inputs are
measurements or assumptions before using a

12
00:00:45.043 --> 00:00:46.520
real feed plan.

13
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:52.937
A document reader can miss a decimal or
fill a blurred row.

14
00:00:52.937 --> 00:00:58.554
In our packing extract, twenty pieces and
thirty pieces give a clear-row

15
00:00:58.554 --> 00:00:59.958
subtotal of fifty.

16
00:00:59.958 --> 00:01:02.299
The full total remains unknown.

17
00:01:02.299 --> 00:01:07.916
Check extracted figures against their
exact rows and obtain a readable source

18
00:01:07.916 --> 00:01:09.320
for missing details.

19
00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:15.240
Different apps may use different dates,
names, periods and units.

20
00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:19.848
Keep original exports and propose
uncertain matches for review.

21
00:01:19.848 --> 00:01:25.992
Check a repeated event before removing a
duplicate from a working copy.

22
00:01:25.992 --> 00:01:32.648
A current snapshot cannot establish a
trend, and an unusual sensor reading needs

23
00:01:32.648 --> 00:01:33.160
investigation.

24
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.805
Before uploading real files, check
permissions and provider terms.

25
00:01:37.805 --> 00:01:41.651
Entered records and derived summaries may
have different rights.

26
00:01:41.651 --> 00:01:45.496
The drift activity is another model output
to question.

27
00:01:45.496 --> 00:01:51.478
It pictures how changes affect a plume,
but it cannot predict the field result

28
00:01:51.478 --> 00:01:52.760
or approve spraying.

29
00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:58.447
The wind comparison starts at an
illustrative fifteen kilometres an hour.

30
00:01:58.447 --> 00:02:01.112
It is not a legal limit.

31
00:02:01.112 --> 00:02:06.443
Check your regional plan, product label
and GROWSAFE guidance for actual work.

32
00:02:06.443 --> 00:02:12.663
Try one change in the lab, describe what
moved, and name the evidence the

33
00:02:12.663 --> 00:02:14.440
picture still cannot supply.
